/// \defgroup grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories W3D draw modules: factory pairs and the light and laser save data
/// \ingroup grp_w3d_rendering
/// \brief Twelve of BFME 2's W3D draw module classes, from W3DBuffDraw to
///        W3DTreeDraw, the factory functions the module factory calls to
///        build them and their module data, and the save functions of
///        W3DLightDraw and W3DLaserDraw.
///
/// A draw module is the client-side part of an object that puts it on
/// screen. It belongs to the object's \c Drawable, not to the logic
/// \c Object (\ref sub_gameclient_drawables), and it is declared in the
/// same INI \c Object block as the logic modules, on a line such as
/// `Draw = W3DLightDraw ModuleTag_Light`. BFME 2 registers more draw
/// modules than the twelve here, for example W3DScriptedModelDraw,
/// W3DHordeModelDraw, W3DStreakDraw and W3DProjectileStreamDraw; this
/// group covers only the classes listed below.
///
/// \par From a module name to a module
/// W3DModuleFactory::init first runs ModuleFactory::init and then
/// registers the W3D draw modules, one call each. Every call passes the
/// module name (spelled like the class), the class's instance factory
/// (\c friend_newModuleInstance), a module data factory
/// (\c friend_newModuleData), the draw module type and the draw interface
/// mask (Zero Hour's \c MODULETYPE_DRAW and \c MODULEINTERFACE_DRAW
/// values). BFME 2's registration call also takes a further function,
/// which Zero Hour's \c addModule does not have. Among the classes here,
/// W3DTreeDraw passes one, W3DFloorDraw and W3DPropDraw pass the same one,
/// and W3DSupplyDraw and W3DQuadrupedDraw pass the same one as
/// W3DHordeModelDraw; the others pass none. What that function is for is
/// not established here.
///
/// The two factories run at different times (\ref sub_rts_model_flow has
/// the whole path):
/// 1. **While INI loads.** ThingTemplate::parseModuleName hands the module
///    line to ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI, which finds the
///    registration and calls its data factory with the INI reader. The data
///    factory allocates the module data, runs its constructor (which sets
///    every field's default) and, when the reader is not null, parses the
///    block into it.
/// 2. **When a drawable is built.** The \c Drawable constructor walks the
///    template's draw module list and builds a draw module for each entry
///    through ModuleFactory::newModule, which calls the registered instance
///    factory with the new drawable and the module data from step 1. It
///    skips an entry when the global draw-module LOD switch is on and that
///    entry's module data asks for a higher game LOD than the current
///    static LOD level, so on low static LOD settings a \c Draw line can
///    produce no module. Zero Hour has the same skip; there the base
///    ModuleData asks for the lowest level and W3DModelDraw's data takes
///    its level from a \c MinLODRequired field.
///
/// \par Which data factory each module uses
/// - **Own field table:** W3DBuffDraw, W3DFloorDraw, W3DLaserDraw,
///   W3DLightDraw, W3DPropDraw, W3DQuadrupedDraw, W3DSupplyDraw,
///   W3DTornadoDraw and W3DTreeDraw. Their data factories call
///   INI::initFromINIMultiProc with the data class's \c buildFieldParse.
///   Three of those functions first add a base class's table:
///   W3DFloorDrawModuleData adds W3DPropDrawModuleData's, and
///   W3DSupplyDrawModuleData and W3DQuadrupedDrawModuleData add
///   W3DModelDrawModuleData's (the model draw fields shared with the other
///   model-based draw modules). The field lists are on the class pages and
///   were read from the retail executable's own field tables, which those
///   \c buildFieldParse functions register.
/// - **No fields:** W3DDefaultDraw, W3DDebrisDraw and W3DRopeDraw are all
///   registered with one shared data factory,
///   W3DDebrisDraw::friend_newModuleData. It builds a small data object
///   and parses the block with no field table, so the block may hold
///   nothing but its \c End (that function's page says what a field line
///   there does). ModuleFactory::init registers the same function for other
///   modules that have no data of their own.
///   Zero Hour has the same function once, as the base class's
///   Module::friend_newModuleData, which all three classes inherit; its
///   comment says the empty parse is there so that an \c End token is
///   required.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - Every instance factory has the same shape: allocate the module with
///   the global \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory) at the class's size,
///   run the constructor with the thing and the module data, and return
///   the pointer as the \c Module base the factory stores. Every data
///   factory does the same for its data and adds the INI step. Where the
///   constructor is a separate function, an exception frame around it
///   frees the block if the constructor throws, as a C++ new-expression
///   requires; the shared field-less data factory constructs its data
///   inline and has no such frame.
/// - Zero Hour generates the same functions inline from the module macros
///   in `Common/Module.h`. There, the module comes from the class's own
///   memory pool; BFME 2's bodies call the global \c operator \c new.
/// - A null INI pointer leaves the data at its constructor defaults; Zero
///   Hour's ThingTemplate::initForLTA passes null when it adds
///   W3DDefaultDraw to a template it builds itself. No BFME 2 caller that
///   passes null is identified here.
/// - Neither factory is called directly: the module factory reaches them
///   only through the stored pointers.
/// - Retail has no symbols for the factories; each is identified by the
///   registration that pairs it with a module name. Zero Hour's
///   macros make \c friend_newModuleData a static member of the module
///   class. The reconstruction spells some data factories on the module
///   class (W3DLightDraw::friend_newModuleData) and others on the data
///   class (W3DPropDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData); the spelling says
///   nothing about the retail code, which has the same shape in both
///   cases.
///
/// \par Where the classes come from
/// W3DDebrisDraw, W3DDefaultDraw, W3DLaserDraw, W3DPropDraw, W3DRopeDraw,
/// W3DSupplyDraw and W3DTreeDraw are Zero Hour classes. W3DBuffDraw,
/// W3DFloorDraw, W3DLightDraw and W3DQuadrupedDraw have BFME 1
/// counterparts in Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree. W3DTornadoDraw appears in
/// neither.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their tags, and every
///   field listed on the class pages (plus the shared model draw fields for
///   W3DSupplyDraw and W3DQuadrupedDraw).
/// - Hard-coded: the module names, which data factory each name uses, and
///   what each module does with its fields. A new kind of draw module
///   needs a new class and a new W3DModuleFactory::init registration.
/// - Draw modules change only what each machine draws, but they are
///   declared in \c Object blocks, so their fields still count toward the
///   INI CRC that multiplayer compares (\ref sub_rts_model_modding).
/// - Saves: a drawable's draw modules save their own state (see
///   W3DLightDraw::xfer and W3DLaserDraw::xfer for two of them;
///   \ref sub_save_load_crc_xfer explains the format). Zero Hour keys each
///   module's saved state by its module tag, so renaming a tag in a mod
///   orphans that state in old saves.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Several of these modules do not draw anything themselves. W3DPropDraw
/// hands its model to the terrain's prop buffer, and in Zero Hour
/// W3DTreeDraw hands its tree to the terrain's tree buffer; the buffers
/// then draw all props or trees in batches (\ref sub_w3d_rendering).
/// W3DLightDraw drives one of the 3D scene's dynamic lights. A renderer
/// replacement has to provide those batch and light services, not only
/// per-object models.
///
/// \see W3DModuleFactory::init, ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI,
///      ModuleFactory::newModule, INI::initFromINIMultiProc,
///      \ref sub_w3d_rendering, \ref sub_objects_modules


/// \class W3DBuffDraw
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories
/// \brief Draw module registered as "W3DBuffDraw", configured by a model
///        name and a pre-draw flag.
///
/// A \c DrawModule subclass (its constructor runs the DrawModule
/// constructor and clears one flag of its own). It has a BFME 1
/// counterpart in Open-BFME-1; it is not in Zero Hour. What it draws, and
/// how it relates to the \c BuffNugget FX nugget, is not established here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of W3DBuffDrawModuleData: \c ModelName (text) and \c PreDraw
/// (yes/no).
/// \see W3DBuffDraw::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      W3DBuffDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class W3DBuffDrawModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for W3DBuffDraw: a model name and a pre-draw flag.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// W3DBuffDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// drawable of the template. The field list is on W3DBuffDraw.

/// \fn Module *W3DBuffDraw::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "W3DBuffDraw": creates the module
///        for one drawable.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when a
/// drawable whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The drawable being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's W3DBuffDrawModuleData, shared with
///                    every other drawable of the template.
/// \return The new module, as the Module base the factory stores.
/// \see W3DBuffDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *W3DBuffDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "W3DBuffDraw": creates the module
///        data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The fields come from
/// W3DBuffDrawModuleData::buildFieldParse, a single table.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data, as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see W3DBuffDraw::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class W3DDebrisDraw
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories
/// \brief Draw module registered as "W3DDebrisDraw": draws a piece of
///        debris whose model and animations are set from code, not from
///        INI.
///
/// Zero Hour: the module also implements \c DebrisDrawInterface, through
/// which the code that creates debris objects sets the model
/// (W3DDebrisDraw::setModelName) and the animation names
/// (W3DDebrisDraw::setAnimNames) after the drawable exists.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The module data has no fields: an INI block naming W3DDebrisDraw takes
/// only its \c End. A field line in it causes a null-pointer read fault
/// rather than the unknown-field error (see
/// W3DDebrisDraw::friend_newModuleData). The model comes from whatever
/// creates the debris (in Zero Hour, an object creation list's debris
/// entry).
/// \see W3DDebrisDraw::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      W3DDebrisDraw::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn Module *W3DDebrisDraw::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "W3DDebrisDraw": creates the
///        module for one drawable.
///
/// Same pattern as W3DBuffDraw::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The drawable being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's field-less module data from
///                    W3DDebrisDraw::friend_newModuleData.
/// \return The new module, as a Module pointer.
/// \see W3DDebrisDraw::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *W3DDebrisDraw::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Field-less data factory that W3DModuleFactory::init registers for
///        "W3DDefaultDraw", "W3DDebrisDraw" and "W3DRopeDraw"
///        (ModuleFactory::init also registers it for other modules that
///        have no data); it requires an empty INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for any module line naming one of those modules.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Allocates, with the global \c operator \c new, a small data object with
/// no INI fields of its own and, when the reader is not null, calls
/// INI::initFromINI with no field table. The object's exact class is not
/// established: it does not match the reconstructed ModuleData constructor
/// (Zero Hour: a plain ModuleData). The function is the counterpart of
/// Zero Hour's Module::friend_newModuleData, which W3DDebrisDraw,
/// W3DDefaultDraw and W3DRopeDraw inherit there. The executable holds one
/// copy, and the reconstruction names it after W3DDebrisDraw. Besides the
/// three W3DModuleFactory::init registrations, ModuleFactory::init
/// registers the same function for logic and client modules that have no
/// data of their own (for example BridgeTowerBehavior, InactiveBody and
/// SwayClientUpdate). The block must contain nothing but its \c End.
/// The null table is not a safe empty list: INI::initFromINI stores it as
/// the only table, and for a line whose first token is not \c End the
/// field lookup reads through that null pointer before it can report the
/// field as unknown. So in BFME 2, as in Zero Hour, a field line in such a
/// block causes a null-pointer read fault instead of the readable
/// unknown-field error that blocks with a field table give. What the user
/// then sees depends on error handling further up, which is not traced
/// here.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null.
/// \return The new module data, as a ModuleData pointer.
/// \see W3DDefaultDraw::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      W3DDebrisDraw::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      W3DRopeDraw::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class W3DDefaultDraw
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories
/// \brief Draw module registered as "W3DDefaultDraw": the placeholder draw
///        module, with no fields.
///
/// BFME 2's constructor only runs the DrawModule constructor, and the
/// instance factory allocates no more than a DrawModule needs, so the
/// class has no per-drawable state of its own. Zero Hour's version, by
/// contrast, keeps a render object, which it loads from the template's
/// test-asset name when the template has one; ThingTemplate::initForLTA
/// adds the module to templates built from test assets, and the partition
/// manager skips ghost objects for immobile objects whose draw module is
/// W3DDefaultDraw.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Its data comes from the shared field-less factory
/// W3DDebrisDraw::friend_newModuleData, so its block takes no fields.
/// \see W3DDefaultDraw::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *W3DDefaultDraw::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "W3DDefaultDraw": creates the
///        module for one drawable.
///
/// Same pattern as W3DBuffDraw::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The drawable being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's field-less module data.
/// \return The new module, as a Module pointer.
/// \see W3DDebrisDraw::friend_newModuleData


/// \class W3DFloorDraw
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories
/// \brief Draw module registered as "W3DFloorDraw": a W3DPropDraw with
///        extra settings for level of detail, draw order, visibility,
///        fading on death, a weather texture and hiding by model condition.
///
/// BFME 2's constructor runs the W3DPropDraw constructor and then sets up
/// four flags of its own, and its data's \c buildFieldParse adds
/// W3DPropDrawModuleData's table before its own, so a W3DFloorDraw block
/// accepts the prop fields too. It has a BFME 1 counterpart in
/// Open-BFME-1; it is not in Zero Hour.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of W3DFloorDrawModuleData, after the W3DPropDraw fields
/// (\c ModelName, \c DistanceFog): \c StaticModelLODMode, \c ForceToBack
/// and \c StartHidden (yes/no each), \c FloorFadeRateOnObjectDeath (real),
/// \c WeatherTexture and \c HideIfModelConditions. The last two have
/// parsers of their own; \c HideIfModelConditions reads a list of model
/// conditions. What each field does is not established here beyond its
/// name.
/// \see W3DFloorDraw::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      W3DFloorDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class W3DFloorDrawModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for W3DFloorDraw: the W3DPropDraw fields plus the
///        floor settings.
///
/// Built by W3DFloorDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData; the field list
/// is on W3DFloorDraw.

/// \fn Module *W3DFloorDraw::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "W3DFloorDraw": creates the
///        module for one drawable.
///
/// Same pattern as W3DBuffDraw::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The drawable being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's shared W3DFloorDrawModuleData.
/// \return The new module, as a Module pointer.
/// \see W3DFloorDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *W3DFloorDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "W3DFloorDraw": creates the module
///        data and parses the INI block into it.
///
/// Same pattern as W3DBuffDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData. The field
/// source, W3DFloorDrawModuleData::buildFieldParse, adds
/// W3DPropDrawModuleData::buildFieldParse's table and then its own.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null.
/// \return The new module data, as a ModuleData pointer.
/// \see W3DFloorDraw::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class W3DLaserDraw
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories
/// \brief Draw module registered as "W3DLaserDraw": draws a laser as one or
///        more beams of segmented lines between two points that a
///        \c LaserUpdate client update module supplies.
///
/// W3DLaserDraw::doDrawModule looks up the drawable's client update module
/// named \c LaserUpdate and does nothing without one. When that update
/// reports that the laser moved, or the module's own dirty flag is set, it
/// rebuilds the beams: straight, or arched over several segments, with
/// widths and colours blended from the inner to the outer beam. While
/// rebuilding it updates a brightness scale from the \c Envelope and
/// assigns each beam the texture at a current index into the \c Texture
/// list; after every draw, rebuilt or not, that index moves on by one and
/// wraps to the start of the list. The constructor loads every listed
/// texture and creates the line objects.
///
/// W3DLaserDraw::loadPostProcess sets the dirty flag after a load, so the
/// beams are rebuilt on the next draw, as in Zero Hour.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of W3DLaserDrawModuleData: \c NumBeams and \c Segments
/// (whole numbers), \c InnerBeamWidth, \c OuterBeamWidth, \c ScrollRate,
/// \c ArcHeight, \c SegmentOverlapRatio, \c TilingScalar and \c FanWidth
/// (reals), \c InnerColor and \c OuterColor (colours with alpha),
/// \c MaxIntensityLifetime and \c FadeLifetime (durations), \c Tile
/// (yes/no), \c Texture (appends to a list of texture names) and
/// \c Envelope (keyword and value pairs on the same line, read by a parser
/// of its own: \c InitialOpacity, \c PeakOpacity and \c SustainOpacity,
/// plus the times \c InitialDelay, \c AttackTime, \c DecayTime,
/// \c SustainTime and \c ReleaseTime in milliseconds; an \c End token may
/// close the list). Zero Hour's table
/// has no \c FanWidth or \c Envelope and reads \c Texture as a single
/// name. An object with this module also needs a \c LaserUpdate client
/// update module.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The texture index advances once per call of the draw function, so a
/// multi-texture laser animates at the draw rate, not at a fixed time
/// step.
/// \see W3DLaserDraw::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      W3DLaserDraw::friend_newModuleData, W3DLaserDraw::xfer

/// \fn Module *W3DLaserDraw::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "W3DLaserDraw": creates the
///        module for one drawable.
///
/// Same pattern as W3DBuffDraw::friend_newModuleInstance; the class page
/// describes what the constructor sets up.
///
/// \param thing       The drawable being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's shared W3DLaserDrawModuleData.
/// \return The new module, as a Module pointer.
/// \see W3DLaserDraw::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *W3DLaserDraw::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "W3DLaserDraw": creates
///        W3DLaserDrawModuleData and parses the INI block into it.
///
/// Same pattern as W3DBuffDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData, with
/// W3DLaserDrawModuleData::buildFieldParse, a single table, as the field
/// source.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null.
/// \return The new module data, as a ModuleData pointer.
/// \see W3DLaserDraw::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn void W3DLaserDraw::xfer(Xfer *xfer)
/// \brief Saves, loads or checksums a laser draw module's animation state.
///
/// The drawable's save code calls it for this module, through the
/// module's snapshot interface, whenever the game is saved or loaded
/// (\ref sub_save_load_crc_xfer).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Transfers, in this order:
/// 1. version 1 (Xfer::Version1);
/// 2. the base module's data (DrawModule::xfer);
/// 3. the brightness scale the envelope updates (a real; the constructor
///    starts it at 1);
/// 4. the module's own copy of \c FanWidth (a real, taken from the module
///    data by the constructor and passed to each beam line);
/// 5. the index of the current texture in the \c Texture list (an
///    integer);
/// 6. the texture aspect ratio (a real: width over height of the last
///    listed texture the constructor loaded, 1 when none loaded).
///
/// Nothing else of the laser is saved: the beam lines, the texture list,
/// the envelope's own state and the dirty flag are not part of the data.
///
/// \param xfer  The transfer in use: saving, loading or checksumming.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's version saves only the version and the base module's
///       data; its comment says there is no data to save. BFME 2 keeps
///       version 1 but adds the four values, so the two formats are not
///       interchangeable even though the version byte is the same.
/// \par Remastering
/// A remaster that wants to load retail saves must read the four values in
/// this order. Adding laser state means raising the version and keeping a
/// path for version 1.
/// \see W3DLaserDraw::loadPostProcess, W3DLightDraw::xfer


/// \class W3DLightDraw
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories
/// \brief Draw module registered as "W3DLightDraw": attaches a dynamic
///        point light to the drawable and animates its intensity with an
///        optional flicker and pulse.
///
/// The constructor takes a dynamic light from the 3D scene's pool
/// (Zero Hour's scene reuses a disabled light or creates a new one), adds
/// a reference to it, enables it and sets its ambient and diffuse colours
/// from the module data, its position to the origin and its far
/// attenuation range from 1 to \c Radius. It also starts the pulse phase at
/// a random value between 0 and 30 (WWMath::Random_Float, not the game
/// logic's random generator), so lights do not pulse in step. The
/// destructor disables the light and releases its reference; it disables
/// it before testing the pointer, so it relies on the constructor having
/// obtained a light.
///
/// W3DLightDraw::doDrawModule runs on each draw:
/// - It advances the pulse phase by the reciprocal of a global frame-rate
///   value (Open-BFME-1's reconstruction of the BFME 1 class uses the
///   constant 0.2 here).
/// - It moves the light to a position read from the drawable, keeping the
///   light's orientation.
/// - It starts from \c Intensity. With \c FlickerAmplitude above zero, a
///   flicker value drifts toward a random target within that amplitude of
///   \c Intensity in equal steps, picking a new target after a number of
///   draws set by the frame-rate value divided by \c FlickerFrequency.
/// - With \c PulseAmplitude above zero, it adds \c PulseAmplitude times
///   the sine of 2 pi times the phase times \c PulseFrequency, so
///   \c PulseFrequency counts full pulses per unit of phase.
/// - It stores the result in the light.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of W3DLightDrawModuleData: \c Ambient, \c Diffuse and
/// \c Specular (RGB colours), \c Radius, \c Intensity,
/// \c FlickerAmplitude, \c FlickerFrequency, \c PulseAmplitude and
/// \c PulseFrequency (reals) and \c AttachToBoneInAnotherModule (a bone
/// name, text). When the drawable's transform changes,
/// W3DLightDraw::reactToTransformChange gives the light the drawable's
/// transform, or the transform of that bone when the field is set and the
/// drawable finds the bone. None of the functions read here uses
/// \c Specular.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The light is a fixed-function dynamic light of the W3D scene, which a
/// new renderer must provide or map. Flicker and pulse advance once per
/// call of the draw function, not by elapsed time, so their speed follows
/// the draw rate and the global frame-rate value.
/// \see W3DLightDraw::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      W3DLightDraw::friend_newModuleData, W3DLightDraw::xfer

/// \fn Module *W3DLightDraw::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "W3DLightDraw": creates the
///        module, and with it a dynamic light, for one drawable.
///
/// Same pattern as W3DBuffDraw::friend_newModuleInstance; the class page
/// describes what the constructor sets up.
///
/// \param thing       The drawable being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's shared W3DLightDrawModuleData.
/// \return The new module, as a Module pointer.
/// \see W3DLightDraw::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *W3DLightDraw::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "W3DLightDraw": creates
///        W3DLightDrawModuleData and parses the INI block into it.
///
/// Same pattern as W3DBuffDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData, with
/// W3DLightDrawModuleData::buildFieldParse, a single table, as the field
/// source.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null.
/// \return The new module data, as a ModuleData pointer.
/// \see W3DLightDraw::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn void W3DLightDraw::xfer(Xfer *xfer)
/// \brief Saves, loads or checksums a light draw module's flicker and pulse
///        state.
///
/// The drawable's save code calls it for this module, through the
/// module's snapshot interface, whenever the game is saved or loaded
/// (\ref sub_save_load_crc_xfer).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Transfers, in this order:
/// 1. version 1 (Xfer::Version1);
/// 2. four reals: the pulse phase, the number of draws left before the
///    flicker picks a new target, the flicker's step per draw and the
///    current flicker value (roles as used by W3DLightDraw::doDrawModule);
/// 3. the base module's data (DrawModule::xfer).
///
/// The light itself and its colours are not saved; the constructor sets
/// them up again from the module data.
///
/// \param xfer  The transfer in use: saving, loading or checksumming.
///
/// \note The class's own values come before the base module's data, the
///       reverse of W3DLaserDraw::xfer (and of Zero Hour's W3DLaserDraw).
///       Either order works as long as saving and loading agree; it matters
///       for anything that reads existing saves.
/// \par Remastering
/// A remaster that wants to load retail saves must keep this order,
/// including the base data after the four values.
/// \see W3DLaserDraw::xfer


/// \class W3DPropDraw
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories
/// \brief Draw module registered as "W3DPropDraw": hands a static model to
///        the terrain's prop buffer once and lets the buffer draw it.
///
/// W3DPropDraw::reactToTransformChange does the work. The first time the
/// drawable's transform changes while its position is away from the map
/// origin (a position whose x and y are both 0 is skipped), it calls
/// BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass::addProp on the terrain render object with
/// the drawable's position and orientation, two further values read from
/// the drawable (Zero Hour: its id and scale), \c ModelName and the
/// \c DistanceFog flag, and remembers that it did; later changes are
/// ignored. BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass::addProp forwards the prop to
/// W3DPropBuffer::addProp. Zero Hour does the same without the flag.
///
/// W3DFloorDraw is built on this class.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of W3DPropDrawModuleData: \c ModelName (text) and
/// \c DistanceFog (yes/no; BFME 2 addition, passed on to the prop buffer
/// with the prop).
/// \see W3DPropDraw::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      W3DPropDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class W3DPropDrawModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for W3DPropDraw: a model name and a distance-fog flag.
///
/// Built by W3DPropDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData; W3DFloorDraw's
/// data parses the same two fields first.

/// \fn Module *W3DPropDraw::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "W3DPropDraw": creates the module
///        for one drawable.
///
/// Same pattern as W3DBuffDraw::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The drawable being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's shared W3DPropDrawModuleData.
/// \return The new module, as a Module pointer.
/// \see W3DPropDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *W3DPropDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "W3DPropDraw": creates the module data
///        and parses the INI block into it.
///
/// Same pattern as W3DBuffDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData, with
/// W3DPropDrawModuleData::buildFieldParse, a single table, as the field
/// source.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null.
/// \return The new module data, as a ModuleData pointer.
/// \see W3DPropDraw::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class W3DQuadrupedDraw
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories
/// \brief Draw module registered as "W3DQuadrupedDraw": a model draw module
///        that also knows a four-legged model's four foot bones.
///
/// BFME 2's constructor runs the W3DScriptedModelDraw constructor, and its
/// data's \c buildFieldParse adds W3DModelDrawModuleData's table before
/// its own, so the block takes every model draw field. The class adds
/// W3DQuadrupedDraw::getFootLocations, which uses the four foot bones to
/// fill a list of positions.
/// It has a BFME 1 counterpart in Open-BFME-1; it is not in Zero Hour.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of the module data, after the model draw fields:
/// \c LeftFrontFootBone, \c RightFrontFootBone, \c LeftRearFootBone and
/// \c RightRearFootBone (bone names, text).
/// \see W3DQuadrupedDraw::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      W3DQuadrupedDraw::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn Module *W3DQuadrupedDraw::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "W3DQuadrupedDraw": creates the
///        module for one drawable.
///
/// Same pattern as W3DBuffDraw::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The drawable being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's shared quadruped module data.
/// \return The new module, as a Module pointer.
/// \see W3DQuadrupedDraw::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *W3DQuadrupedDraw::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "W3DQuadrupedDraw": creates
///        W3DQuadrupedDrawModuleData and parses the INI block into it.
///
/// Same pattern as W3DBuffDrawModuleData::friend_newModuleData. The field
/// source, W3DQuadrupedDrawModuleData::buildFieldParse, adds the model draw
/// table and then the four foot bones.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null.
/// \return The new module data, as a ModuleData pointer.
/// \see W3DQuadrupedDraw::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class W3DRopeDraw
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories
/// \brief Draw module registered as "W3DRopeDraw": draws a rope whose
///        length, speed, width and colour are set from code.
